Prepare indices at stratum boundaries

Compile each stratum's input indices and transitive native transport
dependencies before Rust generation. Prepare the selected indices before
evaluation and pass direct references to rules. Keep simultaneous
scheduling as the default, with one preparation plan per round.

Refresh only the member relations needed by the stratum, including graph
and application dependencies for nested models. Propagate secondary
orders from owned tuples so inherited subtrees remain shared. Refresh
all public views on return and before close_until callbacks.

Retain one typed maintenance state per secondary order and catch up with
borrowed, sharing-aware differences. Keep per-stratum consumption
checkpoints separately so later readers reuse the latest maintained
tree while seeing all changes since their own previous evaluation.
Share typed old/new views between matching checkpoints without type
erasure, and build secondary views only for the ages consumed by rules.
Public queries outside evaluation remain lazy.

Cover separated readers, index selection, late graph edges, cycles,
inherited subtree sharing, equality replacements, and both scheduling
modes. Leave canonicalization-aware branch updates for separate work.
